--- sum takes its start by name
sum() takes no positional arguments — the start is named: `xs.sum(start=n)`
sum() start must not be null — leave `start=` out for the default of 0
sum() got an unexpected keyword argument 'base'
13
--- sort_by takes the function, and takes `reverse=` by name
sort_by() takes exactly 1 argument
sort_by() needs a function, not 'int'
sort_by() reverse must be a bool, not 'int'
sort_by() reverse must be a bool, not 'null'
sort_by() got an unexpected keyword argument 'key'
--- sort_in_place is the same shape, on a list only
sort_in_place() takes exactly 1 argument, the key function (0 given) — `xs.sort_in_place(x => x)` sorts by the elements themselves
sort_in_place() takes exactly 1 argument, the key function (2 given) — `xs.sort_in_place(x => x)` sorts by the elements themselves
sort_in_place() needs a key function, not 'str' — `xs.sort_in_place(x => x)` sorts by the elements themselves
sort_in_place() reverse must be a bool, not 'int'
`sort_in_place` sorts a list where it is, and only a list can be changed that way — `xs.sort_by(f)` returns a sorted copy
`sort_in_place` sorts a list where it is, and only a list can be changed that way — `xs.sort_by(f)` returns a sorted copy
`sort_in_place` sorts a list where it is, and only a list can be changed that way — `xs.sort_by(f)` returns a sorted copy
--- the two cut sorts name their replacement
`sorted` is not in Oro — sort with `xs.sort_by(f)`: `xs.sort_by(x => x)` for the elements' own order, `xs.sort_by(f, reverse=true)` for a stable descending one
`sorted` is not in Oro — sort with `xs.sort_by(f)`: `xs.sort_by(x => x)` for the elements' own order, `xs.sort_by(f, reverse=true)` for a stable descending one
`list.sort` is not in Oro — sort a list where it is with `xs.sort_in_place(f)`: `xs.sort_in_place(x => x)` for the elements' own order, `xs.sort_in_place(f, reverse=true)` for a stable descending one
`list.sort` is not in Oro — sort a list where it is with `xs.sort_in_place(f)`: `xs.sort_in_place(x => x)` for the elements' own order, `xs.sort_in_place(f, reverse=true)` for a stable descending one
a `str` is not a collection in Oro — `s.to_list()` is the bridge into the collection protocol, so write `s.to_list().sort_by(x => x)`
